+### Parameters
+*   $l_p$: Highest positional encoding frequency for position in Main/Smoothing/Lambertian coarse networks.
+*   $l_{ps}$: Highest positional encoding frequency for position in Smoothing coarse network ($l_{ps} < l_p$) [S2].
+*   $e_\tau$: Threshold for reprojection MSE to filter out incorrect depth estimates. Value: Not explicitly given in sources [S1].
+*   $N_c$: Number of coarse samples.
+*   $N_f$: Number of fine samples.
 
-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Specular Regions:** Reprojected patch intensities may not match between views, causing masks to be zero. This results in fewer supervised pixels but prevents incorrect depth supervision [S1, S2].
-*   **Single-View Visibility:** Transparent floaters may persist in regions visible in only one training view because reliable depth supervision (requiring reprojection to a *different* view) is unavailable [S2].
-*   **Poor Base Performance:** The method assumes the base model achieves reasonable performance. It may not help if the base model is very poor (e.g., highly complex scene with very few views) [S2].
-*   **Depth Variance:** The loss is imposed only on mean depth, not variance. Transparent floaters are not fully removed by minimizing variance or KL divergence in experiments [S2].
-
-## Subtopics
-
-### Smoothing Augmentation (Points Augmentation)
-Reduces positional encoding frequencies in the density network to prevent sharp depth discontinuities (floaters). Sets apart by explicitly targeting high-frequency artifacts. Choose when floaters are the primary issue. [S1, S2]
-
-### Lambertian Augmentation (Views Augmentation)
-Disables view-dependent radiance to resolve shape-radiance ambiguity (duplication artifacts). Sets apart by forcing color to be position-only. Choose when specular reflections cause geometric confusion. [S1, S2]
+### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
+*   **Specular Regions:** In specular regions, reprojected patch intensities may not match, causing masks to be zero. This results in no supervision for those pixels, preventing incorrect depth estimates from being applied but reducing the number of supervised pixels [S1, S2].
+*   **Single-View Visibility:** Transparent floaters may persist in regions visible in only one training view because reliable depth supervision (requiring reprojection to a *different* view) is not available there [S2].
+*   **Poor Base Performance:** The method is limited to cases where the base model achieves reasonable performance. It may not help if the base model performance is very poor [S2].
+*   **Depth Variance:** The depth supervision loss is imposed only on the mean depth, not on the variance. Minimizing variance or using KL divergence losses was found to lead to stronger artifacts [S2].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Training takes approximately 1.5 times longer than standard NeRF. This is because augmentations are applied only to the coarse NeRF (192 queries vs 64 for standard coarse), while fine NeRF queries remain at 192. Total queries per pixel: 384 (vs 256 for standard NeRF) [S2].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Increases due to storing parameters for two additional augmented models. Exact VRAM increase not given, but "compute and memory overhead" is noted [S2].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Simple-NeRF queries coarse MLPs 192 times and fine MLPs 192 times per pixel (384 total queries), whereas standard NeRF queries coarse MLPs 64 times and fine MLPs 192 times (256 total) [S2]. Training time is approximately 1.5 times that of standard NeRF [S2].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Increases due to the storage of parameters for two additional augmented models [S2].
 *   **Performance:**
-    *   **LLFF (2 views):** SSIM 0.6501, PSNR 19.57, LPIPS 0.2688. Outperforms RegNeRF and FreeNeRF [S1].
-    *   **LLFF (4 views):** SSIM 0.7016, PSNR 21.99, LPIPS 0.2633. Outperforms RegNeRF and FreeNeRF [S1].
-    *   **RealEstate-10K (2 views):** Depth MAE 0.3269, SROCC 0.9215. Outperforms DS-NeRF [S2].
-*   **Scaling:** Performance improvement reduces as the base model performance saturates and floaters decrease [S2].
-*   **Strengths:** No pre-training required; mitigates both floaters and incorrect depth; handles sparse inputs effectively.
-*   **Limitations:** Limited to cases where base model is reasonably good; ineffective for highly complex scenes with very few views; transparent floaters in single-view regions persist [S2].
+    *   **LLFF (2 views):** SSIM 0.6501, PSNR 19.57, LPIPS 0.2688. Outperforms RegNeRF (SSIM 0.5712, PSNR 18.52, LPIPS 0.3056) and FreeNeRF (SSIM 0.6322, PSNR 19.52, LPIPS 0.2754) [S1].
+    *   **LLFF (4 views):** SSIM 0.7016, PSNR 21.99, LPIPS 0.2633. Outperforms RegNeRF (SSIM 0.6645, PSNR 21.32, LPIPS 0.2794) and FreeNeRF (SSIM 0.6764, PSNR 21.91, LPIPS 0.2848) [S1].
+    *   **RealEstate-10K (2 views):** MAE 0.3269, SROCC 0.9215. Outperforms DS-NeRF (MAE 0.7164, SROCC 0.6660) [S2].
+    *   **NeRF-LLFF (2 views):** MAE 0.1420, SROCC 0.8480. Outperforms DS-NeRF (MAE 0.2074, SROCC 0.7230) [S2].
+*   **Strengths:** Mitigates both floaters and incorrect depth issues; does not require pre-training on large datasets or external depth models; effective in sparse view settings.
+*   **Limitations:** Increased training time and memory; ineffective if base model is very poor; transparent floaters in single-view visible regions; loss only on mean depth.
 
 ## Background
-Simple-NeRF was introduced by Nagabhushan Somraj et al. in 2023 [S1, S2]. It builds on the DS-NeRF baseline [Deng et al. 2022] and addresses the challenges of sparse input view synthesis, specifically overfitting and shape-radiance ambiguity [S1]. The approach is conceptually similar to the Dual-Student architecture [Ke et al. 2019] but uses complementary augmented models rather than identical twins [S1, S2]. It is part of the "Simple Radiance Fields" (Simple-RF) family, which extends these regularization principles to TensoRF and ZipNeRF [S2].
+Simple-NeRF was introduced by Nagabhushan Somraj et al. in 2023 [S1, S2]. It is part of the "Simple Radiance Fields" (Simple-RF) family, which extends the regularization concept to TensoRF and ZipNeRF [S2]. The method builds on the DS-NeRF baseline [S2] and addresses the shape-radiance ambiguity and floater artifacts common in sparse-view NeRF training [S1]. It is conceptually similar to the Dual-Student architecture but uses augmented models with complementary abilities rather than identical replicas [S1, S2].
 
